#!/usr/bin/env bash
# =============================================================================
# CHANGE LOG
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEQ                 | AUTHOR                                     | DESCRIPTION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | Verify the COMMIT, not the working tree, before it reaches main. On 2026-07-14 a hook-rejected commit was retried after a concurrent session cleared the shared index, so it shipped ONE file: the importers of a new module landed while the module itself never did, and main did not compile for ~40 minutes. Nothing caught it because typecheck/tests were green LOCALLY — against files that were on disk but not in git. This is the only check that can catch that class.
# 2 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | Added the PUBLISH GATE as the FIRST step (this repo is now public and has no scrubber in front of it). It runs on every push, including docs-only pushes the typecheck skips, and is NOT bypassable via OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY.
# 3 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | The HEAD typecheck was SILENTLY SKIPPING for every agent-worktree push. REPO_ROOT is `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, which in a LINKED WORKTREE is the worktree itself — and a fresh worktree has no node_modules, so `ln -s "$REPO_ROOT/node_modules"` produced a dangling link (or failed) and the hook printed one stderr line and exited 0. Worktree isolation is now the normal way parallel agents work here, so the default case for automated pushes was ZERO verification — precisely the class SEQ 1 exists to catch, and exactly the "a gate that skips itself is not a gate" failure the hardening doctrine names. Now: resolve the main checkout's node_modules via git-common-dir, fall back to a Windows junction when MSYS refuses symlinks, and BLOCK with an actionable message (naming the documented override) when neither works, instead of passing quietly. Found 2026-07-29 when an agent noticed its own pre-push had self-disabled, then confirmed live: the push of this very fix printed the OLD skip message. core.hooksPath is an ABSOLUTE path to the main checkout, so EVERY linked worktree executes the main checkout's hooks — which is why the fix takes effect only once main is merged AND the main checkout has pulled it, and why a worktree cannot test its own hook edit by pushing.
# 4 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | Made SEQ 2's promise true. OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 exited at the very top of the script, BEFORE the publish gate — so the one documented escape hatch for a slow/unrunnable typecheck also silently disabled the leak wall on a public repo. The skip check now sits after the gate: you may skip the typecheck, never the gate.
# 5 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | SEQ 3 preferred `$REPO_ROOT/node_modules` when present, which in an agent worktree is normally a JUNCTION back to the main checkout. Linking a link makes tsc resolve @types through a doubly-indirect path and emit ~36 spurious TS2742 "inferred type cannot be named without a reference to ../../../node_modules/@types/qs ... likely not portable" errors, so the newly-loud gate BLOCKED a push whose HEAD typechecks clean — SEQ 3 traded a silent skip for a false positive. Measured on the same commit: link-to-link 36 errors, direct link to the real directory 0. Now a linked worktree always resolves the main checkout's real node_modules and only falls back to its own. Found immediately because SEQ 3 made the gate loud, which is the argument for loud gates.
# 6 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | The publish gate now runs with --pre-push and inherits this hook's stdin, where git writes one ref-update line per pushed ref. Without it the gate could only judge HEAD's unpublished commits, and a push BY SHA - the shared checkout's private-index recipe - publishes commits HEAD never reaches; the gate's new model-attribution check needs exactly the pushed set. Nothing else in this hook reads stdin.
# 7 | maintainer@emeraldcoastsystemsgroup.com   | The gate was PERMANENTLY RED from every clone, and in one night three lanes pushed past it with OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 - each correctly judging the failure pre-existing, each re-verifying by hand. A fail-closed gate that teaches everyone to skip it is worse than no gate. Cause: tsconfig sets "declaration": true, so tsc does declaration-emit checking even under --noEmit, and that has to NAME the file every inferred type comes from. node_modules is a LINK in this tree BY DESIGN (you cannot npm install on every push), so tsc resolved the link to its realpath and the only name it could give @types/qs was thirteen ../ back out to the real checkout - TS2742 "cannot be named ... likely not portable" against createApplicationAuthorizationWiring, which returns an express request adapter. SEQ 5 read this signature as link-TO-link; it is not. Measured 2026-09-15 on origin/main 7adc2f02 with a PROVEN single junction straight to the real directory: 1 error, exit 2. The verification tsc now passes --preserveSymlinks, so it names node_modules where it sits in the tree - the layout the real checkout has - and the gate answers the question it is actually asking. It cannot mask a real error: the real node_modules holds ZERO symlinks outside .bin (swept 2026-09-15, 660 packages), so this hook's own link is the only thing the flag can act on; measured against the real @types, the flag is a no-op when node_modules is a real directory (0 errors either way) and a GENUINE TS2742 raised by a transitively nested real package is still reported under it, as are ordinary type errors. Rejected the alternative of switching declaration checking off in the verification run, which would have dropped a whole real diagnostic class - that is the masking this avoids. Guarded by tests/unit/pre-push-preserve-symlinks.spec.ts, which runs the real tsc through a real link.
# =============================================================================
#
# Exports committed HEAD to a scratch tree and typechecks THAT. Rule 0a: one worktree, one index,
# many agents — so what you have is not necessarily what you pushed.
#
# Skip (rare, deliberate): OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 git push
set -uo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || exit 0

# ── PUBLISH GATE (runs FIRST, on every push) ─────────────────────────────────
# This repo is PUBLIC and has no scrubber in front of it. The publish gate refuses
# to push personal/employer/credential content. It runs before — and independently
# of — the typecheck below, because a leak is most likely in a docs or comment
# change, exactly the kind of push the typecheck skips. Not covered by
# OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY: you may skip a slow typecheck, never the leak gate.
# --pre-push hands the gate this hook's stdin (git's ref-update lines), so its commit-message
# checks judge the commits actually being pushed, including a push BY SHA that HEAD never reaches.
if [ -f "${REPO_ROOT}/scripts/publish-gate.sh" ]; then
  if ! bash "${REPO_ROOT}/scripts/publish-gate.sh" --pre-push; then
    echo "" >&2
    echo "✗ pre-push BLOCKED by the publish gate — this repo is PUBLIC (see above)." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

# ── The typecheck escape hatch — deliberately BELOW the publish gate ─────────
# Skipping a slow or unrunnable HEAD typecheck is a supported (rare) choice; skipping the leak
# wall on a public repo is not. This check used to sit at the top of the file, which quietly made
# the flag do both.
if [ "${OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  # Say exactly what was and was not checked. A reader who sees only "SKIPPED" cannot tell whether
  # the leak wall was skipped too, and the whole point of an honest escape hatch is that the next
  # person knows what they are inheriting.
  echo "" >&2
  echo "⚠ pre-push: COMMITTED HEAD WAS NOT TYPECHECKED (OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1)." >&2
  echo "  NOT checked: whether the commits in this push compile TOGETHER. That is the only check that" >&2
  echo "  reads what you pushed rather than what is on your disk, so a green local typecheck says" >&2
  echo "  nothing here — the classic shape is a file present locally but never committed, landing the" >&2
  echo "  importers of a module without the module (Change Log 1)." >&2
  echo "  STILL RAN, and never bypassable by this variable: the publish gate (the leak wall)." >&2
  echo "  You own what lands. If main goes red, it is yours to fix now, not next session." >&2
  echo "" >&2
  exit 0
fi

# Nothing to verify if there are no TS/JS sources in the push range (docs-only pushes are common
# and this hook should not tax them).
RANGE_FILES="$(git diff --name-only "@{push}..HEAD" 2>/dev/null || git diff --name-only HEAD~1..HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
if [ -n "$RANGE_FILES" ] && ! printf '%s\n' "$RANGE_FILES" | grep -qE '\.(ts|tsx|js|mjs|cjs|json)$'; then
  exit 0
fi

VERIFY_DIR="$(mktemp -d 2>/dev/null || echo "/tmp/oshal-prepush-$$")"
cleanup() { rm -rf "$VERIFY_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true; }
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

echo "pre-push: typechecking COMMITTED HEAD (not the working tree)…" >&2
if ! git archive HEAD | tar -x -C "$VERIFY_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
  echo "pre-push: could not export HEAD — allowing push (do not block on tooling failure)." >&2
  exit 0
fi

# node_modules is huge; link rather than copy. A LINKED WORKTREE has no node_modules of its own, so
# resolve the main checkout's via git-common-dir — otherwise every agent-worktree push silently got
# NO verification at all (see Change Log 3). Missing deps now BLOCK instead of passing quietly: a
# gate that skips itself is not a gate.
# Prefer the MAIN checkout's node_modules whenever we are in a linked worktree, even if this
# worktree has one of its own — an agent worktree's node_modules is almost always a junction back
# to the main checkout, and linking a link makes tsc emit ~36 spurious TS2742 "cannot be named
# without a reference to ../../../node_modules/@types/qs, likely not portable" errors. Those are an
# artifact of the doubly-indirect path, not real type errors, and they blocked a push whose HEAD
# typechecks clean. Resolving to the real directory yields zero. (See Change Log 5.)
NODE_MODULES=""
COMMON_DIR="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)"
MAIN_ROOT="${COMMON_DIR%/.git}"
if [ -n "$MAIN_ROOT" ] && [ "$MAIN_ROOT" != "$REPO_ROOT" ] && [ -d "$MAIN_ROOT/node_modules" ]; then
  NODE_MODULES="$MAIN_ROOT/node_modules"
  echo "pre-push: linked worktree — using the main checkout's node_modules ($MAIN_ROOT)" >&2
elif [ -d "$REPO_ROOT/node_modules" ]; then
  NODE_MODULES="$REPO_ROOT/node_modules"
fi

if [ -z "$NODE_MODULES" ]; then
  echo "" >&2
  echo "✗ pre-push BLOCKED — cannot verify committed HEAD: no node_modules found." >&2
  echo "  Looked in this tree and in the main checkout (via git-common-dir)." >&2
  echo "  Run 'npm ci --legacy-peer-deps' in the main checkout, then push again." >&2
  echo "  Override (rare, and you own what lands): OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 git push" >&2
  echo "" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# MSYS/Windows refuses symlinks under some configurations; a directory junction is equivalent here
# and costs nothing. Only if BOTH mechanisms fail do we block.
if ! ln -s "$NODE_MODULES" "$VERIFY_DIR/node_modules" 2>/dev/null; then
  if ! MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 cmd //c mklink /J "$(cygpath -w "$VERIFY_DIR")\\node_modules" "$(cygpath -w "$NODE_MODULES")" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "" >&2
    echo "✗ pre-push BLOCKED — could not link node_modules into the verification tree." >&2
    echo "  Neither 'ln -s' nor 'mklink /J' worked, so committed HEAD cannot be typechecked." >&2
    echo "  Override (rare, and you own what lands): OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 git push" >&2
    echo "" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

# --preserveSymlinks is what makes this tree answer the question the gate is actually asking.
# node_modules is LINKED in here by design, and tsconfig sets "declaration": true, so tsc does
# declaration-emit checking even under --noEmit and has to NAME the file each inferred type comes
# from. Resolving the link to its realpath leaves it able to name @types/qs only by a path that
# climbs out of the verification tree, so it raises TS2742 "likely not portable" against code that
# is fine — a permanent RED that taught three lanes in one night to push with the override. With the
# flag, node_modules is named where it sits in the tree, which is the layout the real checkout has.
# It suppresses no diagnostic: the real node_modules contains no symlinks outside .bin, so this
# hook's own link is the only thing the flag acts on, and a genuine TS2742 from a nested real
# package is still reported under it. (Change Log 7.)
OUT="$(cd "$VERIFY_DIR" && timeout 600 npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit --pretty false --preserveSymlinks 2>&1)"
RC=$?

if [ $RC -ne 0 ]; then
  # A tooling failure (no tsc, timeout) must not block a push; only real type errors do.
  if ! printf '%s' "$OUT" | grep -qE 'error TS[0-9]+'; then
    echo "pre-push: verification could not run (rc=$RC) — allowing push." >&2
    exit 0
  fi
  echo "" >&2
  echo "✗ pre-push BLOCKED — committed HEAD does not typecheck." >&2
  echo "" >&2
  printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | grep -E 'error TS[0-9]+' | head -12 >&2
  echo "" >&2
  echo "  Your working tree may be green while HEAD is not: one worktree, one index, many agents." >&2
  echo "  Most likely a file is on disk but never committed (a hook-rejected commit that was retried" >&2
  echo "  after a concurrent session cleared the index ships only what you re-staged)." >&2
  echo "" >&2
  echo "  Fix: git status --short   → re-add the missing files, amend or commit, push again." >&2
  echo "  Override (rare): OSHAL_SKIP_PREPUSH_VERIFY=1 git push" >&2
  echo "" >&2
  exit 1
fi

echo "pre-push: committed HEAD typechecks ✓" >&2
exit 0
